;;; A keyword pair as a guard's argument reads nothing, in silence — M1e2
;;; **W24**, register row `W24/kwpair-in-guard` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`),
;;; banked by S1e2.2.24 (2026-11-05) from its step 1's probe
;;; `build/s1e2.2.24/shapes/g-kw-rule.ein` (the program unchanged), ruled by
;;; the user on 2026-11-05 (Q-M1e2.18 (iii)(a)).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Four facts of `p`, and one rule whose `eq` guard writes the keyword pair
;;; `:k a` as its second argument. (`neq` is shape-pinned, so `(neq ?x :k a)`
;;; is a parse error; `eq` is a generic list, so this parses.)
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A guard compares two values, and a keyword pair is none. The user ruled
;;; that a keyword pair written as a guard's argument is refused at load,
;;; with a line in Ein's spelling naming the rule and the clause. Every
;;; command that loads the program exits 1 (`ein test`: 2).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ─────────────
;;;
;;; Until S1e2.2.24 the program loaded, and the guard compared the pair as a
;;; raw IR node, equal to no value, so `e` never fired: root held no
;;; `hit-eq`, exit 0, with no word of the pair (`ein saturate --dump FILE`
;;; and `ein solve FILE` at `f8c10730`, release and debug). Since then the
;;; loader refuses it: *kb load error: rule 'e': its :match writes (eq ?x :k
;;; a), and the keyword pair :k a is no value — a guard compares two values,
;;; each a variable a premise before it binds, a name, a string or an
;;; integer*.

(relation p Node) (relation q Node) (relation f Node) (relation hit-eq Node) (relation hit-neq Node)
(p a) (p b) (p 1) (p (f a)) (q a)
(rule e () :match (and (p ?x) (eq ?x :k a)) :assert (hit-eq ?x))
